1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,001
If you spent all weekend building

2
00:00:02,001 --> 00:00:04,852
a twenty-tab productivity dashboard and stopped

3
00:00:04,852 --> 00:00:06,125
using it by Thursday,

4
00:00:06,125 --> 00:00:09,400
a nineteen seventy-five engineering rule explains why.

5
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:11,717
Systems theorist John Gall put it

6
00:00:11,717 --> 00:00:13,823
in his 1975 book Systemantics:

7
00:00:13,823 --> 00:00:16,000
a complex system that works has

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,457
always evolved from a simple system

9
00:00:18,457 --> 00:00:19,300
that worked.

10
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:21,967
A complex system designed from scratch

11
00:00:21,967 --> 00:00:22,810
never works.

12
00:00:22,810 --> 00:00:25,004
Beginners break this rule constantly.

13
00:00:25,004 --> 00:00:27,614
They design twelve-step morning routines and

14
00:00:27,614 --> 00:00:30,520
complicated automation pipelines before they have

15
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:32,300
even shipped a single piece of

16
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:33,130
finished work.

17
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:34,373
Flip the sequence:

18
00:00:34,373 --> 00:00:36,514
start with one plain checklist,

19
00:00:36,514 --> 00:00:38,034
one daily focus block,

20
00:00:38,034 --> 00:00:40,451
and one repeatable workflow you can

21
00:00:40,451 --> 00:00:42,937
execute even on your most exhausting

22
00:00:42,937 --> 00:00:43,490
Tuesday.

23
00:00:43,490 --> 00:00:45,888
Earn the right to add complexity

24
00:00:45,888 --> 00:00:48,286
only after thirty days of simple

25
00:00:48,286 --> 00:00:48,661
wins,

26
00:00:48,661 --> 00:00:49,410
otherwise.
